WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.890
Task statement 3.6.

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:05.036
Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD pipelines.

3
00:00:05.036 --> 00:00:13.449
In CI nobody can answer a prompt, so Claude must run headless, return machine-readable results, and stay low-noise.

4
00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:18.264
In continuous integration nobody sits at the keyboard.

5
00:00:18.264 --> 00:00:24.840
Claude has to run once, produce output a script can read, and exit, just like a linter or a test runner.

6
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.364
Your pipeline then decides what to do with the result, such as posting comments on the pull request or failing a check.

7
00:00:33.764 --> 00:00:38.702
The dash p flag, also spelled dash dash print, is the non interactive mode.

8
00:00:38.702 --> 00:00:43.969
Add output format json to get a JSON object with the result and session details.

9
00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:50.815
Add a JSON schema flag to make the answer follow your own structure, which your script can parse safely.

10
00:00:50.815 --> 00:00:55.951
In current docs the schema result appears in a field called structured output.

11
00:00:57.351 --> 00:00:59.387
Quality in CI comes from context.

12
00:00:59.387 --> 00:01:03.337
A fresh session reviews better than the one that wrote the code.

13
00:01:03.337 --> 00:01:13.828
When a pull request gets new commits, give Claude the earlier findings and ask only for what is new or still unresolved, so developers do not see the same comments again.

14
00:01:13.828 --> 00:01:19.815
When generating tests, point Claude at existing tests and the testing standards in CLAUDE dot md.

15
00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:29.412
In the continuous integration scenario, a pull request opens and the pipeline runs Claude in print mode with a findings schema.

16
00:01:29.412 --> 00:01:31.671
A script posts each finding inline.

17
00:01:31.671 --> 00:01:37.738
On the next push, the pipeline passes the previous findings so Claude reports only new issues.

18
00:01:37.738 --> 00:01:43.999
A separate job generates tests, guided by the existing suite and the team's documented standards.

19
00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:47.013
Beware of invented flags.

20
00:01:47.013 --> 00:01:49.724
The real non interactive switch is dash p.

21
00:01:49.724 --> 00:01:52.887
Beware of letting the author review its own work.

22
00:01:52.887 --> 00:02:02.700
And watch for noise: re-reviewing everything on every push, or generating tests that duplicate what exists, makes developers stop trusting the pipeline.

23
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:11.029
In CI, run Claude with the print flag, ask for JSON, and shape it with a schema so your scripts can act on it.

24
00:02:11.029 --> 00:02:12.667
Review in a fresh session.

25
00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:18.778
Give re-reviews the previous findings, and give test generation the existing tests and standards.

26
00:02:18.778 --> 00:02:21.487
That keeps the pipeline accurate and quiet.

